{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# Class config schema and resolver\n\n## Context / Problem\nrebar has no way to express \"which model for which capability tier\". `cfg.model` is a single scalar,\nso every per-pass decision is inferred (see the parent's problem statement). Nothing can be wired to\nclasses until the classes exist and can be resolved.\n\n## Approach\nAdd three config slots — `trivial`, `standard`, `frontier` — parsed from the EXISTING layered config\n(`config.py:388-398`: CLI `-c llm.KEY` > `REBAR_LLM_<KEY>` env > config file > default). Each slot\naccepts `model`, `provider`, `endpoint`, and an optional `fallback` array; the array is PARSED and\ncarried now but not yet consumed (its runtime wiring is a sibling ticket), so the schema does not need\na second migration later.\n\nExpose one resolver: class name -> provider-qualified model string. It reuses `infer_provider`\n(`config.py:344`) when `provider` is omitted, so a slot naming only `model = \"claude-opus-4-8\"` keeps\nworking exactly as a bare id does today.\n\nSecrets stay env-only: a slot MUST NOT accept an `api_key`, mirroring the existing rule that secrets\nare not config-file keys.\n\n**Per-class defaults, when a slot is unset.** The defaults ARE today's values, so an operator who\nconfigures nothing gets byte-identical behaviour to the current release — which is also this work's\nrollback story:\n\n| class | default | where it comes from today |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `frontier` | `claude-opus-4-8` | `config.py:39` `DEFAULT_MODEL` |\n| `standard` | `claude-sonnet-4-6` | `config.py:45` `VERIFIER_DEFAULT_MODEL`, documented as \"the decisive NON-FRONTIER model\" |\n| `trivial` | `claude-haiku-4-5` | `plan-review.yaml:62-65`, the `model_ladder` entry rung |\n\nEach default is a BARE id, so it resolves through `infer_provider` exactly as it does today. No\ndefault names a provider, so configuring nothing cannot silently pin an operator to one.\n\n**How the flat env layer addresses a structured slot.** `REBAR_LLM_<KEY>` is scalar by construction,\nso a nested slot is addressed by FIELD, one env var per field:\n`REBAR_LLM_FRONTIER_MODEL`, `REBAR_LLM_FRONTIER_PROVIDER`, `REBAR_LLM_FRONTIER_ENDPOINT`, and the\nsame for `STANDARD_` and `TRIVIAL_`. This keeps the existing precedence rule intact per key (CLI >\nenv > config file > default) rather than inventing a merge rule for whole objects.\n\n**The `fallback` sub-object schema.** A fallback entry is a PROVIDER TARGET carrying exactly the same\nfields as a class slot, minus `fallback` itself:\n\n| field | required | type | notes |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `model` | YES | string | a bare id or a provider-qualified one, same as a slot's `model` |\n| `provider` | no | string | omitted -> `infer_provider`, identical to slot behaviour |\n| `endpoint` | no | string | omitted -> the provider's default endpoint |\n| `api_key` | FORBIDDEN | — | rejected loudly, same secrets boundary as the slot |\n\n**It is NON-RECURSIVE**: a fallback entry MUST NOT itself carry a `fallback` key, and one that does is\na typed config error. The chain is already an ordered list, so nesting adds no expressiveness while\nintroducing cycle risk and an unbounded resolution depth. A slot with no `fallback` key and a slot\nwith `fallback = []` are the same thing: no fallbacks.\n\nThis ticket PARSES and VALIDATES that shape and carries it on the resolved record; CONSUMING it at\nruntime is task cc33.\n\n`fallback` is deliberately CONFIG-FILE-ONLY and has no env form, because encoding an array of objects\nin a scalar env var would mean inventing a serialization format. An environment needing a fallback\nchain uses `REBAR_LLM_CONFIG_FILE` — which **does NOT exist yet**; it is delivered by sibling task\ncb6f. Until cb6f lands there is no environment-only way to set a fallback chain, and that is accepted:\nthis ticket's job is the schema, not the delivery mechanism.\n\n**Alternative rejected — a new config file/discovery path.** Forks the precedence chain and duplicates\nthe loader; the `REBAR_LLM_CONFIG_FILE` pointer that cb6f will add is the escape hatch instead.\n\n**THE INTEGRATION POINT — who calls the parser.** `parse_class_slots` takes an already-loaded\nmapping, which leaves open who hands it the real config. That entry point is\n`model_classes.load_class_slots(repo_root=None)`, in the SAME new module:\n\n    def load_class_slots(repo_root=None) -> dict[str, ClassSlot]:\n        from rebar.llm.config import _read_llm_file_table\n        return parse_class_slots(_read_llm_file_table(repo_root).get(\"model_classes\"))\n\n`_read_llm_file_table` (`config.py:401`) already returns the merged `[tool.rebar.llm]` table\n(user < project) and already degrades to `{}` on a malformed core config, so a broken pyproject\ncannot break an LLM op — that failure mode is inherited, not re-invented.\n\nIt lives in `model_classes.py`, NOT in `config.py`, for two reasons: config.py has 23 lines of\nheadroom, and `LLMConfig` does not need to carry the slots — consumers (tasks 172e, 7761, cc33)\ncall `load_class_slots()` directly. Putting it here also keeps the config import edge one-way.\n\nWithout this named entry point the schema would be unreachable from real configuration — a parser\nnothing calls. That is what the second plan review correctly blocked on after the scope was\nnarrowed to keep config.py under its cap.\n\n## Scope\n- `src/rebar/llm/model_classes.py` — NEW leaf module: the three slots, the `fallback` entry schema,\n  their validation, the resolver, AND the `load_class_slots()` entry point that reads the real\n  config table. **Not `config.py`**: that file is at 777 of the 800-LOC hard\n  cap (23 lines of headroom) and this schema is far larger than that, so putting it there is not\n  landable — the same saturation ticket 1484 just relieved elsewhere. The new module imports\n  `infer_provider` / `DEFAULT_MODEL` / `VERIFIER_DEFAULT_MODEL` FROM `config.py` and `config.py` does\n  NOT import it back, so the edge is one-way and no cycle is possible.\n- `src/rebar/llm/config.py` — only the env-var docstring block for the nine new vars, if the\n  generator reads it. Any larger change here means the split above was not respected.\n- `tests/unit/` — new tests for parsing and resolution.\n- `docs/env-vars.md` — REGENERATED in this change. The nine new per-field env vars\n  (`REBAR_LLM_{FRONTIER,STANDARD,TRIVIAL}_{MODEL,PROVIDER,ENDPOINT}`) necessarily appear in the\n  generator's output, so the committed file must be updated here or the drift gate fails.\n\n## Testing\nUnit tier: pure config parsing and string resolution, no model calls, no network.\n- happy path: a slot with model+provider resolves to `provider:model`.\n- omitted provider: resolves via `infer_provider`, matching today's bare-id behaviour.\n- unset slot: resolves to the documented default for that class.\n- a slot carrying `api_key` is REJECTED loudly (the secrets boundary).\n- a `fallback` array parses and is carried on the record without being consumed.\n- a `fallback` entry is validated to the slot-minus-fallback schema: `api_key` rejected, a nested\n  `fallback` rejected, an omitted `provider` inferred.\n- `fallback = []` and an absent `fallback` key produce the same resolved record (no fallbacks).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] a test asserts each of the three class names resolves to a provider-qualified model string\n- [ ] a test asserts that with NO class configured, `frontier`/`standard`/`trivial` resolve to\n      `claude-opus-4-8`/`claude-sonnet-4-6`/`claude-haiku-4-5` respectively — i.e. today's behaviour\n      is preserved bit-for-bit when nothing is configured\n- [ ] a test asserts `REBAR_LLM_FRONTIER_MODEL` overrides the config-file value for that slot's\n      `model` field only, leaving the slot's other fields intact\n- [ ] a test asserts a slot omitting `provider` resolves identically to today's bare-id behaviour\n- [ ] a test asserts a slot containing `api_key` raises a typed rebar config error naming the secrets rule\n- [ ] a test asserts a parsed `fallback` array is carried on the resolved record\n- [ ] a test asserts `load_class_slots()` reads the `model_classes` sub-table from the merged\n      `[tool.rebar.llm]` config and returns resolved slots — proving the parser is REACHABLE from\n      real configuration and not an orphan\n- [ ] a test asserts `load_class_slots()` returns the documented defaults when the config file has\n      no `model_classes` table at all (the inherited degrade-to-empty path)\n- [ ] a test asserts a `fallback` entry carrying `api_key` raises a typed rebar config error (the\n      secrets boundary applies to ENTRIES, not only to slots)\n- [ ] a test asserts a NESTED `fallback` (an entry itself carrying a `fallback` key) raises a typed\n      rebar config error naming the non-recursive rule\n- [ ] a test asserts a `fallback` entry omitting `provider` resolves via `infer_provider`, identically\n      to a slot that omits it\n- [ ] `docs/env-vars.md` is REGENERATED AND COMMITTED in this change, and the regenerated file lists\n      all nine `REBAR_LLM_{FRONTIER,STANDARD,TRIVIAL}_{MODEL,PROVIDER,ENDPOINT}` vars; re-running the\n      generator afterwards then produces no diff, which is what the drift gate actually checks"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785487120536294001,"uuid":"159eddfd-d9ec-4e3c-adb1-59d677f07a01"}